            "markup": "\u003Cp class=\u0022govuk-heading-s govuk-body\u0022\u003EThe TPR header is designed for TPR use, and is\u00A0\u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E based on the \u003Ca href=\u0022https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/header/\u0022\u003Eheader\u003C/a\u003E component of the GOV.UK Design System. It typically appears as shown above, where all of its content areas are in use, and supports localisation / culture variance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp class=\u0022govuk-heading-s govuk-body\u0022\u003EThe TPR header is made up of two components, the header bar and the context bar. The header bar has three areas:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003Ethe TPR logo, which is always present\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003Ea label (optional), which is set to the strapline \u0027Making workplace pensions work\u0027 or the name of a bigger service of which the current service is one part\u00A0\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003Ea space for service-wide links like \u0027Logout\u0027 (optional)\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe context bar also has three areas:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003Econtext 1 has the service name, if the strapline is used. If the header label names a bigger service, context 1 might name the current part of that service or the mode that the service is in. It does not support names that need to wrap.\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003Econtext 2 has the name of the thing being displayed or edited\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003Cli\u003Econtext 3 has the identifier of the thing being displayed or edited\u003C/li\u003E\n\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe context bar is for context that spans multiple pages. The context for the current page, such as the page heading, does not belong in the context bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 class=\u0022govuk-heading-s\u0022\u003EHeader bar with no label\u003C/h2\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
